## What was still missing

The matcher could say whether a pattern matched and where, and nothing else.
That is enough for a predicate and not enough for `re-find`. Of the eleven
regex literals in `kotoba-lang/htmldom`, FOUR exist to pull text out -- the
character-entity forms and the attribute scanner -- and a plain `(...)`
compiled to exactly the same program as `(?:...)`, so the parentheses were
decoration.

## Captures

Opcode 5 `save` writes the current input position into a slot, so a thread
carries slots and the thread entry widens from `pc(6)` to `pc(6) + nslots*6`.

The width is a PARAMETER, not a branch. A program with no `save` has nslots 0
and width 6, which is byte for byte the entry the VM had before. That is the
claim the unchanged 270-check `vm_parity` and 168-check `emit_parity` stand
behind: the capture-free path is not a special case, it is nslots = 0.

`save` costs no input, so it lives in the epsilon closure with split, jmp and
the anchors. `run-from` now answers a string rather than an integer, because a
match has to carry its slots out and there is one return value.

nslots is scanned from the code rather than added to the header. Widening the
header would invalidate every program literal already compiled and committed
-- kiyaku carries six -- and the scan is bounded by the instruction count. A
format is a public surface even when it lives inside one repo.

In the emitter, a capturing `(` emits `save(2(g-1))` before and
`save(2(g-1)+1)` after; `(?:` emits neither. The group NUMBER is the source's,
counted by a scan rather than threaded through the records, because a group
number does not depend on anything downstream. The scan skips `(` inside a
character class and after a backslash, and both are asserted.

## The semantics, said out loud rather than assumed

This is a Pike VM: leftmost-LONGEST (POSIX). JavaScript backtracks:
leftmost-FIRST (Perl). On `(a|ab)` against \"ab\" this takes \"ab\" and JavaScript
takes \"a\", and both are right for their semantics.

So `test/capture_parity.cljs` counts an extent disagreement as a semantic
difference and fails only on a GROUP disagreement within an agreed extent --
and asserts the divergence in the direction it exists. Over the 60 corpus
patterns that have groups: 1277/1277 agree, and extent differences are ZERO.
That zero is the interesting number, because preferring the shortest match
instead makes 86 of the same checks diverge: the corpus is not blind to match
length, it simply never distinguishes longest from first. The probe covers the
one case that does.

## What it bought, measured rather than claimed

All eleven of htmldom's regex literals now compile, including the three-group
entity form and the four-group attribute scanner at 37 instructions -- which
is past the 32-item document ceiling that the string program was built for in
the first place. Run against JavaScript on the two capture regexes with real
HTML fragments: 15/15 identical, including non-participating groups reported
as -1 by both and a match found at a non-zero offset.

`test/expressible.cljs` makes that a standing measurement rather than a note.
It reports the goal-level question -- can the browser stack be written in
Kotoba at all, and if not WHICH FEATURE is missing -- because \"16 refused\" is
not actionable and this is:

  expressible: 168/184   not a regex (scrape artifact): 6
    :group/inline-flags 5        (?m) (?s) (?is)   -- cssom
    :quantifier/non-greedy 3     *? +?             -- cssom, css
    :refused/lookahead 2         (?=) (?<=)        -- cssom

The useful part is what is NOT in that list. **htmldom is fully expressible.
The three remaining gaps are cssom's, and they are named.**

The probe ratchets in both directions: a gap that grows is over it, and so is
a gap the table still claims that no longer occurs -- otherwise the table
quietly keeps room it does not need, which is how a ratchet stops measuring.
The six scraped strings that are not regexes at all are reported but not
ratcheted, because their number tracks the scrape rather than the library.

## Two reds that were rejected

Both breaks tried first were reds for the WRONG reason, and neither was
accepted as a demonstration. Reading past the verdict crashed instead of
disagreeing. Flipping the `deeper`/`here` preference does not compile at all:
`deeper` is eagerly bound, so the compiler refuses a `let` that would recurse
on the branch guarding against it -- a refusal worth recording, because it
means that particular mistake cannot be made silently.

The breaks that were accepted: `group-start` reading the end slot (1170/1277,
group boundaries disagree) and preferring the shortest match (1276/1277, ONLY
the divergence probe).

## Left standing

`kotoba-lang/amu`'s shared checkout is 14 commits behind its own west pin with
another process writing into it every few minutes, so the `kotoba` on PATH is
still pre-#912. The work above ran from a worktree at the merged commit. That
checkout is not this session's to stash; the commit it carries is preserved as
`refs/preserved/amu-rank-tick-355` and its working tree is archived under
`.git/stash-archive-20260909/`.

Also: the `jv/expressible` branch was force-pushed once, to replace a stub
commit message before the PR existed. The branch was one commit old, unmerged
and unshared, but the rule is unconditional and the amend should have happened
before the first push.

## What this does not say

It does not say htmldom is migrated. It says the reason it could not be has
been removed and measured away, and that the next repo in the dependency order
needs three named features rather than an unknown number."}]
